    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VI of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlfonsoVI_of_Castile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded by MaiDireLollo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Portugal grew out of the County of Portugal, the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minho-river">Minho</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mondego">Mondego</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of León</a> granted in the 1090s to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, husband of his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-countess-of-portugal">Teresa</a>. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> defeated his mother’s party at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sao-mamede">Battle of São Mamede</a> in 1128 and took control of the county, ruling first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guimaraes">Guimarães</a> and then from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. After a victory over Muslim forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ourique">Battle of Ourique</a> in 1139, whose scale was greatly enlarged by later legend, he began to call himself king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León</a> accepted the royal title at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zamora">Treaty of Zamora</a> in 1143, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a> as king in the bull <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manifestis-probatum">Manifestis probatum</a></em> of 1179. The new kingdom expanded south at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>: with the help of English, Flemish and German crusaders bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, Afonso took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> after a siege in 1147, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-portugal">Santarém</a> fell the same year. The frontier then moved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a> with the help of the military orders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-iii-of-portugal">Afonso III</a> completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faro">Faro</a> in 1249, and the Treaty of Alcañices with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1297 fixed a land border that has changed very little since, making Portugal one of the oldest stable states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lisbon.jpg" alt="Lisbon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisboa_-_Portugal_(52597836992).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitor Oliveira from Torres Vedras, PORTUGAL, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Portugal was a small agrarian kingdom of perhaps a million people, divided between a populous, densely farmed north of small holdings and a south of large estates held by the crown, the church and the military orders. Its towns governed themselves through charters, the <em>forais</em>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> grew into Atlantic ports exporting wine, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, fish and cork to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-of-portugal">King Denis</a> (r. 1279–1325) encouraged farming and trade, founded a university in Lisbon in 1290, later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, and promoted Portuguese as the language of royal documents. The fifteenth-century voyages transformed this economy. Gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a>, enslaved Africans, and from 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> brought by the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-da-india">Casa da Índia</a> made Lisbon one of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Brazilian sugar dominated the seventeenth century, and the gold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, found in the 1690s, flowed through Lisbon in the eighteenth. Society remained strongly Catholic and hierarchical. Manuel I forced the conversion of the kingdom’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1497, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Portuguese Inquisition</a>, founded in 1536, pursued these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a> for two centuries. Much colonial wealth passed through Portugal to northern Europe, while the kingdom’s own manufacturing stayed weak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota.jpg" alt="Battle of Aljubarrota" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota">Battle of Aljubarrota</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalha_de_Aljubarrota_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean d'Wavrin (Chronique d'Angleterre), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early kingdom was made by war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, fought by royal hosts, town militias and the military orders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Hospitallers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-aviz">Avis</a>, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-christ-portugal">Order of Christ</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-of-portugal">Denis</a> founded in 1319 to take over the Templars’ property. Its independence was tested above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. During the crisis of 1383–1385 John, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">Master of Avis</a>, and his constable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-alvares-pereira">Nuno Álvares Pereira</a> defeated a much larger Castilian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota">Battle of Aljubarrota</a> on 14 August 1385, with the help of English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1386">Treaty of Windsor</a> of 1386 began an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that still exists. Overseas power rested on ships and guns: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">caravels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrack">carracks</a> armed with artillery let small fleets dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> in 1509, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> in 1510, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> in 1515. Disaster came in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian</a> died with much of the nobility at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578. After 1640 the Restoration War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ameixial">Ameixial</a> in 1663 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montes-claros">Montes Claros</a> in 1665. Portugal was later occupied by French armies from 1807 and defended with British help, notably behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lines-of-torres-vedras">Lines of Torres Vedras</a> in 1810.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ordenacoes-afonsinas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ordenacoes-afonsinas.jpg" alt="Ordenações Afonsinas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ordenacoes-afonsinas">Ordenações Afonsinas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%BAcleo_Antigo_C%C3%B3dice_14,_book_V,_folio_1_(recto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Portugal was a hereditary Catholic monarchy whose kings ruled through a royal council, a chancery and royal judges, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> brought together clergy, nobility and representatives of the towns, which first sat together at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiria">Leiria</a> in 1254. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a> voted taxes and acted at moments of crisis, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in 1385, when the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-das-regras">João das Regras</a> argued for the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">John I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>. Royal law was codified in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordenacoes-afonsinas">Ordenações Afonsinas</a></em> of the 1440s, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline</a> and Philippine ordinances. Overseas, the crown kept trade with Asia as a royal monopoly run through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-da-india">Casa da Índia</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, and it divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> into captaincies from the 1530s. From 1580 to 1640 Portugal shared its kings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-tomar">Cortes of Tomar</a> in 1581 guaranteed its separate laws and offices. After the Braganza restoration the Cortes ceased to meet after 1698, and gold revenues let John V govern without them. Under Joseph I the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> ran an enlightened absolutism from 1750 to 1777, reforming finance and education and expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1759. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution of 1820</a> brought constitutional rule, settled by the Constitutional Charter of 1826, which with amendments lasted until 1910.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery.jpg" alt="Jerónimos Monastery" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1502_legte_K%C3%B6nig_Manuel_I._den_Grundstein_f%C3%BCr_das_Hieronymus_Kloster_in_Lissabon._06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Holger Uwe Schmitt, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Portugal opened the sea routes that joined the continents. Under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a> its sailors settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>, passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador">Cape Bojador</a> in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> rounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> in 1488. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1498, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1500, and by mid-century Portuguese traders were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494, which divided the newly found lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, shaped the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> that grew from these voyages lasted until 1999, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> was handed to China, and left Portuguese as the language of Brazil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and other countries, today spoken by more than 250 million people. It also opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> on a large scale: Portuguese and Brazilian ships carried more enslaved Africans across the Atlantic than those of any other nation. Portuguese culture produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline style</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-belem">Tower of Belém</a>, the epic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a></em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a>, published in 1572, and advances in navigation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a>. The alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the oldest in force in the world, became a lasting part of European diplomacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir.jpg" alt="Battle of Alcácer Quibir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lagos46_kopie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Jansoone, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Alcácer Quibir</a> without an heir opened a succession crisis, and in 1580 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> made good his claim by invasion, beginning sixty years of union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> during which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> attacked Portuguese posts in Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. A revolt on 1 December 1640 placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a> on the throne as John IV, and Spain recognised independence by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lisbon-1668">Treaty of Lisbon</a> in 1668. Brazilian gold financed a revival, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> earthquake of 1 November 1755 destroyed much of the capital. In 1807, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-andoche-junot">Junot</a> advanced, the royal court sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, and in 1815 the realm became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>. Brazil declared independence in 1822, and the kingdom was then torn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-wars">Liberal Wars</a> of 1828–1834 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-iv-of-kongo">Pedro IV</a> and his brother Miguel. Later decades of rotating parties and debt were followed by the British Ultimatum of 1890, which ended Portuguese claims between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and discredited the monarchy. King Carlos I and his heir were assassinated in Lisbon on 1 February 1908. On 5 October 1910 a republican revolution deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-of-portugal">Manuel II</a>, who went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the First Portuguese Republic replaced the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Portugal kept borders that have barely changed since 1297 and, from a small Atlantic base, opened sea routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, creating the first global maritime empire and spreading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-language">Portuguese language</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> across several continents.</em></p>

